WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:02.530
- Michael Avenatti and
his client Stormy Daniels

00:00:02.530 --> 00:00:04.270
lost one of their defamation suits

00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:06.220
against President Trump and will now

00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:08.790
have to pay Trump's attorney's fees.

00:00:08.790 --> 00:00:10.760
Let's talk about why.

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:13.427
(twinkly music)

00:00:15.410 --> 00:00:18.330
Hey Legal Eagles, let's
think like a lawyer.

00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:20.270
Today we're gonna talk
about defamation law

00:00:20.270 --> 00:00:22.740
and one of my favorite laws in California,

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:24.610
the Anti-SLAPP statute.

00:00:24.610 --> 00:00:28.140
Yes, lawyers actually have favorite laws.

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:29.980
Today we're gonna cover three things.

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:33.210
First, what in the word is an Anti-SLAPP?

00:00:33.210 --> 00:00:35.670
Number two, why did
Avenatti and Cliffords,

00:00:35.670 --> 00:00:39.490
AKA Stormy Daniels, lose this
particular defamation lawsuit

00:00:39.490 --> 00:00:42.390
and number three, how much
will Avenatti and Cliffords

00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:44.640
have to pay President Trump?

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:47.130
As always, hit that bell
and leave an objection

00:00:47.130 --> 00:00:48.780
if you disagree with me in the comments

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:51.600
and let me know what legal
topic I should cover next.

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:54.510
And as usual, Stella, the legal beagle

00:00:54.510 --> 00:00:56.170
is reading all of your comments.

00:00:56.170 --> 00:00:58.263
So please keep things Stella-safe.

00:00:59.150 --> 00:01:02.360
Alright, so onto the
the Anti-SLAPP statute.

00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:05.020
Anti-SLAPP statutes exist to protect

00:01:05.020 --> 00:01:07.780
and act in furtherance of
someone's right to petition

00:01:07.780 --> 00:01:11.600
or free speech in connection
with a public issue.

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:13.890
The bottom line is that legislatures know

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:15.620
that some people will use litigation

00:01:15.620 --> 00:01:18.010
as a sword instead of shield

00:01:18.010 --> 00:01:21.540
because they know that litigation
is very, very expensive.

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:24.210
A lawsuit can be used
to censor certain people

00:01:24.210 --> 00:01:26.010
if used in the wrong hands.

00:01:26.010 --> 00:01:28.130
So for example, if you
didn't like a Yelp review

00:01:28.130 --> 00:01:30.440
or someone's opinion, you might sue them

00:01:30.440 --> 00:01:32.500
and even if you lost that lawsuit,

00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:34.670
you might be able to inflict huge pain

00:01:34.670 --> 00:01:37.022
and force them to remove their speech

00:01:37.022 --> 00:01:39.970
by forcing them to incur huge legal fees.

00:01:39.970 --> 00:01:41.940
So in response, many states passed

00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:43.710
what are called Anti-SLAPP statutes.

00:01:43.710 --> 00:01:46.060
SLAPP stands for Strategic Lawsuit

00:01:46.060 --> 00:01:47.930
Against Public Participation.

00:01:47.930 --> 00:01:49.990
So, Anti-SLAPP, you wanna prevent people

00:01:49.990 --> 00:01:53.600
from stifling speech by
using strategic lawsuits.

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:55.660
California has one of the strongest.

00:01:55.660 --> 00:01:57.247
For complicated reasons I won't go into

00:01:57.247 --> 00:01:59.100
the court in this particular case

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:02.360
used the Texas statute
but most of the analysis

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:03.600
remains the same.

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:06.740
So an anti-SLAPP motion
is presented as a special

00:02:06.740 --> 00:02:08.140
motion to strike.

00:02:08.140 --> 00:02:11.440
When you file an anti-SLAPP
motion it stops the case,

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:12.930
they force the plaintiff to show

00:02:12.930 --> 00:02:15.290
that the complaint is
both legally sufficient

00:02:15.290 --> 00:02:17.750
and supported by prima facie evidence.

00:02:17.750 --> 00:02:19.710
Usually the plaintiff
doesn't have that burden.

00:02:19.710 --> 00:02:22.110
They can simply file a lawsuit by showing

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:24.430
that their claim has legal merit.

00:02:24.430 --> 00:02:26.770
Not that there is supporting
evidence necessarily,

00:02:26.770 --> 00:02:27.940
that's what discovery is for.

00:02:27.940 --> 00:02:30.420
By the way links to all of these documents

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:32.960
including Judge Otero's
decision in this case

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:35.290
will be down in the description below.

00:02:35.290 --> 00:02:37.447
As well as a lot of the relevant law.

00:02:37.447 --> 00:02:39.530
But, now here's where it gets

00:02:39.530 --> 00:02:42.270
particularly interesting
from my perspective.

00:02:42.270 --> 00:02:45.390
If you file an anti-SLAPP
motion and the court rules

00:02:45.390 --> 00:02:49.330
in your favor, it will both
dismiss the plaintiff's case

00:02:49.330 --> 00:02:51.640
but it will also award the defendant

00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:54.000
attorney's fees and court costs.

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:57.480
The court fees are mandatory
and they are not discretionary.

00:02:57.480 --> 00:03:00.040
In the American system usually, regardless

00:03:00.040 --> 00:03:02.060
of who wins the case both the plaintiff

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:04.670
and the defendant have
to pay for their own fees

00:03:04.670 --> 00:03:07.180
but if you lose an anti-SLAPP motion

00:03:07.180 --> 00:03:10.170
which means the court says,
you filed a strategic lawsuit

00:03:10.170 --> 00:03:12.250
for the purposes of stifling speech,

00:03:12.250 --> 00:03:14.410
then you as the plaintiff have to pay

00:03:14.410 --> 00:03:17.010
for the legal fees of the defendant.

00:03:17.010 --> 00:03:20.240
So that can be a great
reason why as a defendant

00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:21.990
you would want to file
one of these motions

00:03:21.990 --> 00:03:24.800
because you can get attorney's
fees from the plaintiff.

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:27.940
So let's dig in to the facts of this case.

00:03:27.940 --> 00:03:30.550
Now based on the procedural
posture of this case,

00:03:30.550 --> 00:03:34.190
the court is assuming the
plaintiff's facts to be true.

00:03:34.190 --> 00:03:37.870
Now that's only for this very
discrete part of the case.

00:03:37.870 --> 00:03:40.930
It's testing to see if the facts as given

00:03:40.930 --> 00:03:43.340
by the plaintiff state a legal claim.

00:03:43.340 --> 00:03:46.250
If the case went forward, then Clifford,

00:03:46.250 --> 00:03:48.540
AKA Stormy Daniels would have to prove

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:51.910
her allegations with
facts but at the moment,

00:03:51.910 --> 00:03:54.730
the court is taking these
allegations as true.

00:03:54.730 --> 00:03:57.340
So according to her complaint,
Miss Clifford alleges

00:03:57.340 --> 00:03:59.970
that she began an intimate relationship

00:03:59.970 --> 00:04:02.850
with Mr. Trump in 2006.

00:04:02.850 --> 00:04:06.540
Five years later she
claims that in May of 2011

00:04:06.540 --> 00:04:09.330
she agreed to cooperate
with In Touch Magazine

00:04:09.330 --> 00:04:13.210
to talk about her relationship
with Donald Trump.

00:04:13.210 --> 00:04:14.770
She apparently agreed
to speak to the magazine

00:04:14.770 --> 00:04:16.940
after her ex-boyfriend
approached the magazine

00:04:16.940 --> 00:04:18.500
without her approval.

00:04:18.500 --> 00:04:20.860
Clifford alleges that a
few weeks after she agreed

00:04:20.860 --> 00:04:24.160
to talk to In Touch
Magazine, an unidentified man

00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:26.750
approached her and
threatened her in Las Vegas.

00:04:26.750 --> 00:04:29.060
Clifford alleges that
the man approached her,

00:04:29.060 --> 00:04:31.567
threatened her daughter
and told her to quote,

00:04:31.567 --> 00:04:34.134
"Leave Trump alone and forget the story."

00:04:34.134 --> 00:04:37.280
Then, after Mr. Trump
was elected President,

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:39.930
Clifford worked with a
sketch artist to provide

00:04:39.930 --> 00:04:42.754
a sketch of what she
remembered the undisclosed man

00:04:42.754 --> 00:04:45.341
looked like and release
that to the public.

00:04:45.341 --> 00:04:48.100
The day after Mr. Trump tweeted out

00:04:48.100 --> 00:04:50.920
from his Twitter account
at @realDonaldTrump,

00:04:50.920 --> 00:04:53.407
stating quote "A sketch years later

00:04:53.407 --> 00:04:54.697
"about a nonexistent man.

00:04:54.697 --> 00:04:57.807
"A total con job, playing
the Fake News Media

00:04:57.807 --> 00:05:00.740
"for Fools, but they know it."

00:05:00.740 --> 00:05:03.450
Exclamation point, Trump posted this Tweet

00:05:03.450 --> 00:05:06.220
in response to another
Tweet which compared

00:05:06.220 --> 00:05:08.470
the sketch that Miss Clifford released

00:05:08.470 --> 00:05:11.840
to a photo of her
ex-boyfriend side by side.

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:13.710
According to Clifford's argument

00:05:13.710 --> 00:05:16.702
she claims that Trump meant
to convey that she was a liar,

00:05:16.702 --> 00:05:18.480
that she could not be trusted

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:21.010
and she was falsely
complaining about crimes

00:05:21.010 --> 00:05:22.300
that never happened.

00:05:22.300 --> 00:05:25.265
On the basis of Trump's
Tweet she contended

00:05:25.265 --> 00:05:28.820
that it was false, it
constituted defamation

00:05:28.820 --> 00:05:31.060
and that it constituted defamation per se,

00:05:31.060 --> 00:05:34.350
because it charged her with
committing a serious crime.

00:05:34.350 --> 00:05:36.810
Now all of this is
pending in federal court

00:05:36.810 --> 00:05:38.820
in the Central District of California

00:05:38.820 --> 00:05:41.200
which is located partially in Los Angeles

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:44.860
and this is before Federal
Court Judge James Otero.

00:05:44.860 --> 00:05:48.000
Judge Otero is a smart,
very capable judge.

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:50.010
I actually externed on the 9th Circuit

00:05:50.010 --> 00:05:51.730
which is the court directly above

00:05:51.730 --> 00:05:53.460
the Central District of California.

00:05:53.460 --> 00:05:56.560
Literally next door to
Judge Otero's chambers.

00:05:56.560 --> 00:06:00.330
So I used to know Judge
Otero's clerks very, very well.

00:06:00.330 --> 00:06:03.550
So let's talk about Judge Otero's analysis

00:06:03.550 --> 00:06:05.470
in this particular case.

00:06:05.470 --> 00:06:08.580
There is a two-part analysis
that the court must go over.

00:06:08.580 --> 00:06:11.720
First, is to determine
whether the defamation suit

00:06:11.720 --> 00:06:14.930
that Miss Clifford filed is related

00:06:14.930 --> 00:06:18.620
to President Trump's free speech
and public participation?.

00:06:18.620 --> 00:06:20.840
Here, the court had no problem determining

00:06:20.840 --> 00:06:23.740
that it was in fact related
to Trump's free speech.

00:06:23.740 --> 00:06:26.720
No surprise there, this is
quintessential free speech,

00:06:26.720 --> 00:06:30.830
Twitter is quickly becoming
this generation's public square.

00:06:30.830 --> 00:06:32.610
So it's no surprise that the court found

00:06:32.610 --> 00:06:35.610
not only free speech but
public participation,

00:06:35.610 --> 00:06:37.610
almost anything that
relates to the President

00:06:37.610 --> 00:06:40.670
should probably be considered
public participation

00:06:40.670 --> 00:06:43.550
for the purposes of an anti-SLAPP suit.

00:06:43.550 --> 00:06:46.060
So, that takes us to the second question.

00:06:46.060 --> 00:06:49.380
Did Cliffords establish a
prima facie case of defamation

00:06:49.380 --> 00:06:51.556
and the court said she did not.

00:06:51.556 --> 00:06:55.118
So the court agreed with
President Trump's argument

00:06:55.118 --> 00:06:59.940
that the Tweet in question
constituted rhetorical hyperbole

00:06:59.940 --> 00:07:02.730
and rhetorical hyperbole,
normally associated with

00:07:02.730 --> 00:07:05.405
politics and public discourse
is one of those things

00:07:05.405 --> 00:07:08.330
that cannot constitute defamation.

00:07:08.330 --> 00:07:11.070
The First Amendment often protects opinion

00:07:11.070 --> 00:07:15.050
and rhetorical flourishes
and extravagant exaggeration

00:07:15.050 --> 00:07:17.290
used for rhetorical effect.

00:07:17.290 --> 00:07:20.250
And the court found that
President Trump's Tweet

00:07:20.250 --> 00:07:22.843
was exactly that kind
of rhetorical language.

00:07:22.843 --> 00:07:27.370
A published statement that
is pointed or exaggerated

00:07:27.370 --> 00:07:30.520
or heavily-laden with emotional rhetoric

00:07:30.520 --> 00:07:34.560
or moral outrage cannot
generally constitute

00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:36.410
a defamatory statement.

00:07:36.410 --> 00:07:38.890
Now the context particularly matters.

00:07:38.890 --> 00:07:42.059
In filings before the court
and in a public setting,

00:07:42.059 --> 00:07:45.060
Miss Clifford challenged the legitimacy

00:07:45.060 --> 00:07:48.070
of President Trump's victory in 2016.

00:07:48.070 --> 00:07:50.560
Now you can agree with
those statements or not

00:07:50.560 --> 00:07:52.540
but it's pretty clear
that she presented herself

00:07:52.540 --> 00:07:55.710
as an adversary to the
President and the court found,

00:07:55.710 --> 00:07:58.010
I think correctly, that Trump's Tweet

00:07:58.010 --> 00:08:01.171
served as a public
rejoinder, a contradiction

00:08:01.171 --> 00:08:04.139
of the claims that Clifford was making

00:08:04.139 --> 00:08:06.340
against the President.

00:08:06.340 --> 00:08:08.880
To me, and I think a lot
of First Amendment lawyers,

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:10.860
this seems like the right outcome.

00:08:10.860 --> 00:08:13.390
You might agree or
disagree with the President

00:08:13.390 --> 00:08:16.230
but it's important to have
a marketplace of ideas

00:08:16.230 --> 00:08:19.440
and opinions and as
Judge Otero pointed out

00:08:19.440 --> 00:08:21.087
in his decision quote,

00:08:21.087 --> 00:08:23.037
"If this court were to prevent Mr. Trump

00:08:23.037 --> 00:08:25.727
"from engaging in this type
of rhetorical hyperbole

00:08:25.727 --> 00:08:27.737
"against a political adversary,

00:08:27.737 --> 00:08:30.727
"it would significantly hamper
the office of the President.

00:08:30.727 --> 00:08:33.557
"Any strongly-worded
response by a President

00:08:33.557 --> 00:08:35.947
"to another politician or public figure,

00:08:35.947 --> 00:08:38.527
"could constitute an
action for defamation.

00:08:38.527 --> 00:08:41.317
"This would deprive the
country of discourse

00:08:41.317 --> 00:08:43.930
"common to the political process."

00:08:43.930 --> 00:08:44.860
I think that's right.

00:08:44.860 --> 00:08:48.380
The next political opinion to
be silenced could be your own.

00:08:48.380 --> 00:08:51.680
The point of the anti-SLAPP
statute is to prevent lawsuits

00:08:51.680 --> 00:08:53.720
from silencing the little guy

00:08:53.720 --> 00:08:56.980
and while Trump obviously
doesn't fit the definition

00:08:56.980 --> 00:08:57.980
of the little guy,

00:08:57.980 --> 00:09:00.520
for many people legal
fees can be crushing.

00:09:00.520 --> 00:09:02.820
So you may not like
certain political rhetoric,

00:09:02.820 --> 00:09:05.800
but on balance, we're
better off with more speech

00:09:05.800 --> 00:09:08.389
and not less and we certainly
don't want to bankrupt people

00:09:08.389 --> 00:09:10.470
for their political opinions.

00:09:10.470 --> 00:09:13.470
So I think that's why this
was a relatively easy decision

00:09:13.470 --> 00:09:14.560
for Judge Otero.

00:09:14.560 --> 00:09:16.950
This is a pretty clear
case of political rhetoric

00:09:16.950 --> 00:09:18.630
and rhetorical hyperbole.

00:09:18.630 --> 00:09:20.950
This is one of those rare defamation wins

00:09:20.950 --> 00:09:23.810
for President Trump who
often threatens to file

00:09:23.810 --> 00:09:27.490
his own defamation suits
but seldom actually does.

00:09:27.490 --> 00:09:30.520
And although he usually
rails against defamation

00:09:30.520 --> 00:09:32.530
and libel laws in this country,

00:09:32.530 --> 00:09:35.300
he was actually able to use
those against the plaintiff

00:09:35.300 --> 00:09:36.760
in this particular case.

00:09:36.760 --> 00:09:39.186
Now moments before I started filing this

00:09:39.186 --> 00:09:41.187
President Trump tweeted out quote,

00:09:41.187 --> 00:09:43.770
"Federal judge throws out Stormy Daniels,"

00:09:43.770 --> 00:09:46.287
misspelled, "lawsuit versus Trump.

00:09:46.287 --> 00:09:48.710
"Trump is entitled to full legal fees."

00:09:48.710 --> 00:09:52.217
End quote, "@FoxNews,
great, now I can go after

00:09:52.217 --> 00:09:53.997
"Horseface and her third-rate lawyer

00:09:53.997 --> 00:09:55.677
"in the great state of Texas.

00:09:55.677 --> 00:09:57.728
"She will confirm the letter she signed.

00:09:57.728 --> 00:10:01.170
"She knows nothing about me, a total con."

00:10:01.170 --> 00:10:03.560
You know, it's statements like
this that make it really hard

00:10:03.560 --> 00:10:05.730
to defend political
speech but I still think

00:10:05.730 --> 00:10:08.350
the court got it right
in this particular case

00:10:08.350 --> 00:10:11.650
but I should point out that
Trump's tweet is not correct.

00:10:11.650 --> 00:10:14.620
This ruling has nothing to
do with the sister litigation

00:10:14.620 --> 00:10:17.308
over the Michael Cohen
non-disclosure agreement

00:10:17.308 --> 00:10:20.013
or the other cases
involving Michael Cohen,

00:10:20.013 --> 00:10:23.240
President Trump, Stormy
Daniels and Michael Avenatti.

00:10:23.240 --> 00:10:26.970
This was just the discrete
case of defamation

00:10:26.970 --> 00:10:31.610
over the 2018 Tweet which
implied that Stormy Daniels

00:10:31.610 --> 00:10:35.529
was a liar and a quote, total con job.

00:10:35.529 --> 00:10:38.990
It also has nothing to do
with how much Cliffords

00:10:38.990 --> 00:10:41.120
does or does not know about the President

00:10:41.120 --> 00:10:44.300
or whether she engaged
in a sexual relationship

00:10:44.300 --> 00:10:47.510
which I think at this point is
a matter of public knowledge.

00:10:47.510 --> 00:10:50.590
So even in victory, the
President gets a lot of

00:10:50.590 --> 00:10:52.672
the legal aspects of this wrong.

00:10:52.672 --> 00:10:55.603
That's not surprising,
this is complicated stuff.

00:10:55.603 --> 00:10:58.950
Okay, so that takes us
to point number three.

00:10:58.950 --> 00:11:01.810
How much is this going
to cost Stormy Daniels?

00:11:01.810 --> 00:11:03.800
Here, the fees are mandatory

00:11:03.800 --> 00:11:06.410
and Trump's lawyers are expensive.

00:11:06.410 --> 00:11:08.680
The court will generally
pay for reasonable

00:11:08.680 --> 00:11:11.300
attorney's fees but 9 times out of 10

00:11:11.300 --> 00:11:13.670
the court will generally look to the fees

00:11:13.670 --> 00:11:17.000
that the defendant actually incurred

00:11:17.000 --> 00:11:19.467
and will order the plaintiff to pay those.

00:11:19.467 --> 00:11:23.240
But again, this ruling only
applies to this specific

00:11:23.240 --> 00:11:26.840
defamation case and not
the three to six other

00:11:26.840 --> 00:11:28.840
Cohen, Trump, Clifford matters

00:11:28.840 --> 00:11:31.649
that are before Judge Otero
and are still pending.

00:11:31.649 --> 00:11:34.270
So even through this case was stopped

00:11:34.270 --> 00:11:36.100
in the very early early stages

00:11:36.100 --> 00:11:38.520
it still required lots
of different attorneys

00:11:38.520 --> 00:11:40.820
on both sides, lots of legal briefing

00:11:40.820 --> 00:11:42.480
and a ton of argument.

00:11:42.480 --> 00:11:45.610
Thus Trump's lawyers are
likely to ask for and get,

00:11:45.610 --> 00:11:50.610
somewhere between 50 and
$100,000 from Stephanie Clifford.

00:11:50.930 --> 00:11:52.060
So do you agree?

00:11:52.060 --> 00:11:54.870
Please leave your objections
in the comments below

00:11:54.870 --> 00:11:58.143
and until next time,
I'll see you in court.

